<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_em-challenge" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Challenge - Electromagnetism</title>
	
	<!--
	If you want an A*R*C*S (Analyze-Represent-Calculate-Sensemake) activity, use the <project> tag.
	If you want an Explain activity, use the <investigation> tag.
		Use this text for qualitative comparisons: <em>greater than</em>, <em>less than</em>, or <em>equal to</em>
	If you want a Calculate activity, use the <exploration> tag.
	
	<m># \mathrm{~}</m>
	<m> \mathrm{~}</m>
	-->
		<subsubsection>
		<title>Explanation Tasks</title>

	<investigation>
		<title>Plane Wave I</title>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			An electromagnetic plane wave with amplitude <m>\hat{y}</m> with wavelength <m>\lambda</m> is traveling through a vacuum in the positive <m>x</m>-direction.
		</p>
		<p>
			Sketch and label a quantitatively accurate vector map showing both the electric and magnetic fields in the <m>xz</m>-plane at <m>t = 0</m>.  Indicate the direction of travel and the frequency.
		</p>
	</investigation>

		</subsubsection>


	<subsubsection>
		<title>A*R*C*S Activities</title>
	
	<project>
		<title>Plane Wave II</title>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			Consider the plane wave from the previous activity from the perspective of a different reference frame moving in the <m>+x</m>-direction at speed <m>v</m> relative to the original reference frame.
		</p>
		<p>
			Find the electric and magnetic fields in the new reference frame.
		</p>
	</project>
		
</subsubsection>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Metacognitive Reflection</title>
		<activity>
		<title>Inconsistencies</title>
		
		
			<statement>
				<p></p>
			<p>
				You have now observed multiple <em>inconsistencies</em> in physical laws, along with resolutions to these inconsistencies like the introduction of the displacement current or field transformations.  Briefly discuss the importance of identifying and resolving inconsistencies in science.
			</p>
		</statement>
		</activity>

	</subsubsection>

<!--NO AI-->
</section>